/// \defgroup grp_w1018_gui_listbox_combobox_api Gadget API: list box and combo box contents, selection and item data
/// \ingroup grp_gui_apt
/// \brief The small C-style functions that screens call to clear, fill, query and
///        select list boxes and combo boxes, plus the push button label
///        setter.
///
/// List boxes and combo boxes are gadgets of the legacy window system: a
/// GameWindow with a gadget style bit, a block of gadget-specific user data
/// and a system callback that does the real work (see
/// \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets for the gadget model and
/// \ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists for the list box data model). Screen code
/// mostly leaves that user data alone and calls the functions in this
/// group instead. Most of them are
/// one-line wrappers that send the gadget a system message through
/// TheWindowManager (GameWindowManager::winSendSystemMsg) and let
/// GadgetListBoxSystem or GadgetComboBoxSystem act on it.
///
/// Most callers are BFME 2 screen and in-game interface code. Gadget and
/// window code calls some of them too; these callers include
/// GadgetComboBoxSystem, GadgetComboBoxSetText, the combo box factory
/// GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetComboBox, a push button factory in the
/// window manager code, GadgetListBoxSetColors and setWindowText in the
/// window layout loader. Other callers include
/// the multiplayer setup and rules screens
/// (AptMpGameSetup::PopulatePlayerCombo,
/// AptMpGameRules::PopulateComboBoxes, AptMpGameRules::UpdateRuleGadget),
/// the options screen (AptOptions::InitGadgets, AptOptions::Save), the
/// score screen, the create-a-hero screen, the chat windows
/// (ChatWindowsInGame::PopulatePlayerList), the save and load list
/// (GameState::populateSaveGameListbox), the control bar
/// (ControlBar::setControlCommand, for button labels) and
/// InGameUI::hideIdleWorkerLayout (which clears a button label).
///
/// \par Two families of function
/// - Message wrappers: GadgetListBoxReset(), GadgetListBoxGetSelected(),
///   both GadgetListBoxSetSelected() overloads, both
///   GadgetListBoxAddEntryImage() overloads, every combo box function here
///   and GadgetButtonSetText(). They return at once for a null gadget
///   (except the two GadgetListBoxAddEntryImage() overloads, which rely on
///   the send ignoring a null window), then send one
///   message whose two data words carry the arguments, often as the address
///   of a local. The handler runs synchronously inside the send, so those
///   addresses stay valid.
/// - Direct readers and writers: GadgetListBoxGetNumEntries(),
///   GadgetListBoxGetNumColumns(), GadgetListBoxGetColumnWidth(),
///   GadgetListBoxSetAudioFeedback(), GadgetListBoxGetListLength() and
///   GadgetListBoxGetTopVisibleEntry() read or write the list box data
///   directly. All but GadgetListBoxGetListLength() return 0 (or do
///   nothing) when the window or its data is missing.
///
/// \par Message numbering differs from Zero Hour
/// BFME 2's list box and combo box handlers switch on message numbers that
/// sit later than Zero Hour's GadgetGameMessage enum gives: the list box
/// messages are two values later, GCM_ADD_ENTRY is two values later and
/// the combo box messages after it three values later, because BFME 2
/// inserts extra messages, among them a combo box message (right after
/// GCM_ADD_ENTRY) that replaces one row's text.
/// The generic messages used here (GGM_SET_LABEL, GGM_CLOSE) keep their
/// Zero Hour values. This rests on the BFME 2 handlers and wrappers and
/// on Zero Hour's enum. Code ported from Zero Hour that sends these
/// messages itself, rather than through these wrappers, must use BFME 2's
/// numbers.
///
/// \par Null and destroyed windows
/// BFME 2's GameWindowManager::winSendSystemMsg ignores a null window and,
/// for any message other than GWM_DESTROY, a window already marked
/// destroyed, and returns MSG_IGNORED, which is 0. None of the wrappers in
/// this group sends GWM_DESTROY, so for all of them both cases are ignored.
/// A wrapper that returns the handler's result therefore returns 0, not -1,
/// in those cases. GadgetComboBoxAddEntry() is the exception: it returns -1
/// itself for a null combo box.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - A list box or combo box defined in a .wnd layout gets its fixed
///   properties from the layout. BFME 2's parseListboxData() reads, in
///   order (Zero Hour's label names): LENGTH, the list length; AUTOSCROLL;
///   an optional ScrollIfAtEnd flag; AUTOPURGE; SCROLLBAR; MULTISELECT;
///   COLUMNS, with one width per column when there is more than one; and
///   FORCESELECT. AUTOPURGE is the setting the GadgetListBoxAddEntryImage()
///   descriptions refer to: when a list at its capacity needs a new row, one
///   that auto-purges drops its first (top) row, row 0, and moves the rest
///   up; one that does not fails the add. Row 0 is the oldest row only in a
///   list filled purely by appending, since a non-overwriting text add can
///   insert a row in front of an existing one (see
///   GadgetListBoxAddEntryText()). The parser
///   and BFME 2's add handler use the same member of the list box data;
///   Zero Hour copies the parsed data into the list box it creates. BFME 2's
///   parseComboBoxData() reads, in order: the editable flag, the maximum
///   number of characters, the maximum number of entries shown before
///   scrolling, an ASCII-only flag and a letters-and-numbers-only flag
///   (BFME 2 stores the last two as bits of one flags word). See
///   \ref grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers for both parsers. The rows
///   themselves never come from the layout: screen code adds them with
///   GadgetListBoxAddEntryText(), GadgetListBoxAddEntryImage() and
///   GadgetComboBoxAddEntry(). Which entries a combo box offers (player templates, colours, teams, game
///   rules, resolutions) is decided by the screen code that fills it, and
///   the strings come from whatever the caller passes.
/// - Screens attach a pointer-sized value to each combo box row with
///   GadgetComboBoxSetItemData() and read it back after a selection; the
///   multiplayer game rules screen, for example, takes a rule's value from
///   the selected row's item data, and AptMpGameRules::UpdateRuleGadget
///   selects the row whose item data equals the current value. A mod that
///   adds or reorders rows keeps working as long as each row carries the
///   right item data.
/// - These functions change only window state. They are not part of the
///   logic simulation and take no part in lockstep, the logic random
///   numbers or CRC checks; a selection reaches the game only through the
///   screen code that reads it.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - BFME 2's list box image handler (addImageEntry, reached through
///   GadgetListBoxAddEntryImage()) scales an image cell's requested width
///   and height from a 1024 by 768 layout to the current screen resolution
///   held in the global data. Zero Hour stores the requested size as given.
///   Within this group it is the only resolution-dependent step; row
///   heights for text still come from font metrics (see
///   \ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists).
/// - Message data words are 32 bits wide, and several wrappers pass
///   pointers through them (the address of a selection index, of an
///   UnicodeString, of an add-request record, an item data pointer).
///   GadgetListBoxGetSelected() on a multi-select list box even returns a
///   pointer through an Int. A 64-bit port must make the message data type
///   pointer-sized and give that query a pointer-sized output.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The function bodies come from Zero Hour's GadgetListBox.cpp,
/// GadgetComboBox.cpp, GadgetPushButton.cpp and GadgetSlider.h, most of
/// them through the BFME 1 reconstruction. Statements about what a message
/// does in BFME 2 rest on BFME 2's own GadgetListBoxSystem and
/// GadgetComboBoxSystem bodies; statements labelled Zero Hour rest on the
/// Zero Hour source only.
///
/// \see \ref sub_gui_apt, \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets,
///      \ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists,
///      \ref grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers


/// \file Gadget/GadgetListBox.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_gui_listbox_combobox_api
/// \brief List box queries, selection, reset and image rows, and the combo
///        box reset, selection, item data and hide-list wrappers.
///
/// Despite its name this file also holds several combo box message
/// wrappers (reset, selection, item data and hide-list) and a slider
/// colour getter used by GadgetListBoxSetColors().
/// Other list box and combo box functions live in their own files (see
/// \ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists and \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets).


/// \fn void GadgetListBoxReset(GameWindow *listbox)
/// \brief Empties a list box: removes every row, clears the selection and
///        scrolls back to the top.
///
/// Screens call this before refilling a list. Retail callers include
/// GameState::populateSaveGameListbox, ChatWindowsInGame::PopulatePlayerList,
/// AptDisconnectScreen::InitGadgets and GadgetComboBoxSystem, which resets
/// a combo box's list this way.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Sends GLM_DEL_ALL (Zero Hour's name) with both data words zero. BFME 2's
/// list box handler then, for every row up to the list length, frees the
/// display strings of text cells, clears each cell's item data and deletes
/// the row's cell array; zeroes the row array; resets the display position
/// to the top; clears the selection (every slot of a multi-select list, or
/// the single selected index) to -1; sets the insert position, the entry
/// count and the total height to zero; and refreshes the display and scroll
/// bar. The list length (capacity) is unchanged.
///
/// A first data word of GP_DONT_UPDATE (Zero Hour's name; this wrapper
/// never passes it) only skips the direct reset of the display position.
/// The display update that follows recomputes the position for the now
/// empty list, so the view still returns to the top. Zero Hour's handler
/// ends with the same result.
///
/// \param listbox The list box; null does nothing.
///
/// \note The item data of each cell is only cleared, never freed: a screen
/// that stored allocated objects as item data must free them itself before
/// resetting.
/// \see GadgetComboBoxReset(), GadgetListBoxAddEntryText()


/// \fn void GadgetComboBoxReset(GameWindow *comboBox)
/// \brief Empties a combo box: clears its list and its edit text, and
///        disables the drop-down button.
///
/// The usual first step when a screen refills a combo box. Retail callers
/// include AptMpGameSetup::PopulatePlayerTemplateCombo,
/// AptMpGameRules::PopulateComboBoxes, AptOptions::InitGadgets and
/// AptMpClans::PopulateMyClans.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Sends GCM_DEL_ALL (Zero Hour's name) with both data words zero. BFME 2's
/// combo box handler resets the child list box with GadgetListBoxReset(),
/// sets the edit box text to the empty string, sets the entry count to
/// zero and disables the drop-down button. The last step is new in BFME 2;
/// Zero Hour's handler leaves the button alone. GadgetComboBoxAddEntry()
/// enables the button again once there is more than one entry, provided
/// the list box is enabled.
///
/// \param comboBox The combo box; null does nothing.
///
/// \note The name is carried from the BFME 1 reconstruction and its call
/// sites; the BFME 2 body and the message it sends agree with it.
/// \see GadgetComboBoxAddEntry(), GadgetListBoxReset()


/// \fn Int GadgetListBoxGetNumEntries(GameWindow *listbox)
/// \brief Returns how many rows of a list box are in use.
///
/// This is the entry count (Zero Hour's endPos), not the capacity; the
/// capacity is the list length that GadgetListBoxGetListLength() reports
/// for multi-select lists. Callers such as AptMpGameSetup::PopulateColorCombo
/// and the chat windows use it to bound loops over rows.
///
/// \param listbox The list box; may be null.
/// \return The number of rows in use, or 0 when the window or its list box
///         data is missing.
/// \see GadgetListBoxGetListLength(), GadgetListBoxGetNumColumns()


/// \fn void GadgetListBoxSetAudioFeedback(GameWindow *listbox, Bool enable)
/// \brief Turns a list box's click sound flag on or off.
///
/// Writes the flag Zero Hour calls audioFeedback in the list box data.
/// Retail callers include GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetComboBox, which
/// turns it on for the list box it creates inside every combo box, as Zero
/// Hour's factory does.
///
/// Zero Hour: when the flag is set, the list box's input callbacks play the
/// GUIComboBoxClick audio event on mouse clicks and some key presses. How
/// BFME 2's input callbacks use the flag is not established here.
///
/// \param listbox The list box; null does nothing.
/// \param enable  True to enable the feedback.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Zero Hour: the sound is an audio event looked up by name, so its sound
/// file and volume come from the audio event definitions, not from code.


/// \fn Int GadgetListBoxGetNumColumns(GameWindow *listbox)
/// \brief Returns the number of columns in a list box.
///
/// The column count is set when the list box is created (from the
/// LISTBOXDATA block of a .wnd layout, see parseListboxData()) and
/// changed by GadgetListBoxSetColumnWidths.
/// ChatWindowsInGame::PopulatePlayerList checks for a one-column list and
/// gives it its own multi-column layout before filling it.
///
/// \param listbox The list box; may be null.
/// \return The column count, or 0 when the window or its list box data is
///         missing.
/// \see GadgetListBoxGetColumnWidth()


/// \fn Int GadgetListBoxGetColumnWidth(GameWindow *listbox, Int column)
/// \brief Returns the width in pixels of one list box column.
///
/// Reads the list box's array of column pixel widths. Zero Hour computes
/// those widths from the percentage widths given at creation and the list
/// box's width; that derivation is not established for BFME 2 here. In
/// Zero Hour, map list code uses the width to fit an icon into column 0.
///
/// \param listbox The list box; may be null.
/// \param column  The zero-based column index.
/// \return The column's width, or 0 when the window or its data is
///         missing or the column is out of range (negative, or not less
///         than the column count).
/// \see GadgetListBoxGetNumColumns()


/// \fn Int GadgetListBoxGetListLength(GameWindow *listbox)
/// \brief Returns how many selections a caller must be ready to receive:
///        the list length for a multi-select list box, otherwise 1.
///
/// Despite the name, this is not the entry count
/// (GadgetListBoxGetNumEntries()) and, for a single-select list, not the
/// capacity either. Zero Hour's chat code uses it as the loop bound when
/// walking the selection array of its multi-select player list.
///
/// \param listbox The list box; must not be null and must have list box
///        data, since the function checks neither.
/// \return The list length (the maximum number of rows) when the list box
///         is multi-select, else 1.
///
/// \warning Unlike its neighbours this function has no null checks, in
/// BFME 2 as in Zero Hour.
/// \see GadgetListBoxGetSelected()


/// \fn Int GadgetListBoxGetTopVisibleEntry(GameWindow *window)
/// \brief Returns the index of the first row shown at the top of a list
///        box's visible area.
///
/// Screens that rebuild a list call this before GadgetListBoxReset() and
/// restore the scroll position afterwards with
/// GadgetListBoxSetTopVisibleEntry: ChatWindowsInGame::PopulatePlayerList
/// does exactly that, and the other chat windows' player lists call it too.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// After the null checks the function hands the list box data to an
/// internal worker (Zero Hour: getListboxTopEntry). The worker returns the
/// first row whose bottom edge lies below the top of the visible area, that
/// is, the first row at least partly visible, or 0 when it reaches the
/// last row in use without finding one.
///
/// \param window The list box; may be null.
/// \return The top visible row, or 0 when the window or its data is missing
///         or nothing is scrolled into view.
/// \see GadgetListBoxGetNumEntries()


/// \fn void GadgetListBoxGetSelected(GameWindow *listbox, Int *selectList)
/// \brief Reads a list box's selection: the selected row of a
///        single-select list, or the selection array of a multi-select one.
///
/// Retail callers include GadgetComboBoxSystem (answering
/// GadgetComboBoxGetSelectedPos()) and the online login screen
/// (AptOnline::Login::AcceptLocale).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Sends GLM_GET_SELECTION (Zero Hour's name) with selectList as the second
/// data word. BFME 2's handler writes one Int through it:
/// - single-select: the selected row, or -1 when nothing is selected;
/// - multi-select: the address of the list's own selection array, cast to
///   Int. The array holds selected row indices and ends at the first -1.
///   The caller must therefore pass the address of an Int pointer, not of
///   an Int array; the pointer leads to the list box's own storage, not a
///   copy. The in-game chat window reads its multi-select player list
///   this way.
///
/// Both behaviours are the same in Zero Hour, whose comment on the
/// function in GadgetListBox.cpp describes the multi-select case as
/// returning an array.
///
/// \param listbox    The list box; null does nothing and leaves the output
///                   unwritten, so initialise it first.
/// \param selectList [out] Where the selection is written, as described.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The multi-select result is a pointer squeezed into an Int, which only
/// works on a 32-bit build.
/// \see GadgetListBoxSetSelected(GameWindow *, Int),
///      GadgetListBoxGetListLength(), GadgetComboBoxGetSelectedPos()


/// \fn void GadgetListBoxSetSelected(GameWindow *listbox, Int selectIndex)
/// \brief Selects one row of a list box, or clears the selection when the
///        index is out of range.
///
/// Retail callers include MapSelectMenuSystem, GadgetComboBoxSetText and
/// GadgetComboBoxSystem, which forwards GadgetComboBoxSetSelectedPos() and
/// clears its list's selection with -1 when the edit text changes.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Sends GLM_SET_SELECTION (Zero Hour's name) with the address of the
/// index and a count of 1. In BFME 2's handler:
/// - An index below 0 or not below the list length clears the selection
///   and sends GLM_SELECTED to the list box's owner (with -1 for a
///   single-select list).
/// - Single-select list: an index whose row has no cells (an empty row) is
///   ignored. Otherwise the row becomes the selection. If the list box's
///   parent is a combo box, the owner receives GLM_SELECTED with the new
///   index; this is how a combo box learns of a selection. Otherwise BFME 2
///   scrolls the view so that the selected row is shown (an internal helper
///   centres it where the list allows) and sends no notification.
/// - Multi-select list: the handler takes its multi-select path (see
///   GadgetListBoxSetSelected(GameWindow *, const Int *, Int)). An empty
///   row, or a list with no rows in use, leaves the selection empty (the
///   stored selection starts with -1). Otherwise the row becomes the whole
///   selection, followed by the -1 end marker. Either way BFME 2 neither
///   scrolls nor checks for a combo box parent, and sends no GLM_SELECTED.
///
/// \param listbox     The list box; null does nothing.
/// \param selectIndex The row to select, or -1 to clear the selection.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's handler sends GLM_SELECTED to the owner after every
/// valid selection. BFME 2's sends it after a valid selection only for a
/// single-select list box that belongs to a combo box (it still sends it
/// when an out-of-range index clears the selection). Code that selects a
/// row of a plain list box therefore gets no GLM_SELECTED back and must
/// apply any follow-up itself. This rests on both handlers' bodies.
/// \see GadgetListBoxSetSelected(GameWindow *, const Int *, Int),
///      GadgetListBoxGetSelected(), GadgetComboBoxSetSelectedPos()


/// \fn void GadgetListBoxSetSelected(GameWindow *listbox, const Int *selectList, Int selectCount)
/// \brief Sets the selection of a list box from an array of row indices;
///        the form a multi-select list box needs.
///
/// Callers include code in the multiplayer game setup screen
/// (AptMpGameSetup).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Sends GLM_SET_SELECTION (Zero Hour's name) with the array and the count.
/// BFME 2's handler, like Zero Hour's:
/// - If the first index is below 0 or not below the list length, it clears
///   the whole selection and notifies the owner with GLM_SELECTED.
/// - For a multi-select list it copies indices in order, stopping at the
///   count, at the number of rows in use, at an index not less than the
///   list length or at a row with no cells, and ends the stored selection
///   with -1. Rows after a rejected index are dropped, not skipped. Only
///   the first index is checked for being negative.
/// - For a single-select list only the first index is used, as in the
///   single-index overload.
///
/// \param listbox     The list box; null does nothing.
/// \param selectList  The row indices to select; must hold at least one
///                    element, which the handler always reads.
/// \param selectCount How many indices the array holds (Zero Hour's
///                    declaration defaults it to 1).
///
/// \note BFME 2 sends no GLM_SELECTED after a valid multi-select update;
/// Zero Hour does (see the single-index overload).
/// \see GadgetListBoxSetSelected(GameWindow *, Int)


/// \fn Int GadgetListBoxAddEntryImage(GameWindow *listbox, const Image *image, Int row, Int column, Int hight, Int width, Bool overwrite, Int color)
/// \brief Puts an image into a list box cell, appending a new row or
///        writing into an existing one, with an explicit image size.
///
/// The image counterpart of GadgetListBoxAddEntryText(). Retail callers
/// include AptCreateAHero::Manager::PopulateHeroList,
/// ChatWindowsOnline::InsertPlayer and the score screen's list of
/// persistent units.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The function fills an add request (row, column, a pointer to the image,
/// the image type tag, the overwrite flag, width and height) on the stack
/// and sends GLM_ADD_ENTRY (Zero Hour's name) with the request's address
/// and the colour. The list box handler shared with text entries
/// (GadgetListBoxSystem) treats a row at or past the current insert
/// position as an append. When the list is at its capacity and the add is
/// an append or a non-overwriting add to an existing row, an auto-purging
/// list sends itself GLM_SCROLL_BUFFER (Zero Hour's name) with a count of
/// 1, which frees the first row, row 0, and moves the others up; a list
/// that does not auto-purge fails the add. The requested row index is not
/// adjusted for that purge. The handler then calls addImageEntry, which:
/// - rejects a column not less than the column count or a row not less
///   than the list length, returning -1;
/// - for row -1 appends a row at the insert position; for column -1 uses
///   column 0;
/// - allocates the row's cells on first use and frees a text cell's
///   display string before replacing it with the image;
/// - stores the image, the colour and the size, then recomputes every row
///   height (computeTotalHeight()).
/// BFME 2's addImageEntry scales the width by the screen width over 1024
/// and the height by the screen height over 768 before storing them; Zero
/// Hour stores them unchanged. computeTotalHeight() works per cell and
/// gives each row the height of its tallest cell: this image cell counts as
/// its stored height plus 1 when that height is positive, and otherwise as
/// one line of the list box font. Other cells of the same row, such as
/// wrapped text in another column, can still make the row taller.
/// Unless the add was refused because the list was full and does not
/// auto-purge, the handler then runs the auto-scroll and selection
/// follow-up that it shares with text adds. It does this even when
/// addImageEntry rejected the row or column and returned -1. The
/// scroll-if-at-end step of
/// GadgetListBoxAddEntryText() belongs to that wrapper, so image adds do
/// not get it.
///
/// \param listbox   The list box. Not null-checked here, but a null window
///                  makes the send return 0.
/// \param image     The image to show; the cell keeps the pointer, so the
///                  image must outlive the row.
/// \param row       The row to write, or -1 to append.
/// \param column    The column to write, or -1 for column 0.
/// \param hight     The image height in 1024 by 768 layout pixels (the
///                  spelling is Zero Hour's). After scaling, a stored
///                  height of zero or less makes the cell count as one line
///                  of the list box font in computeTotalHeight(); the row
///                  still takes the height of its tallest cell. A small
///                  positive value can scale to zero on a screen under 768
///                  pixels high (see the note for drawing).
/// \param width     The image width in 1024 by 768 layout pixels, scaled
///                  the same way to the screen width. computeTotalHeight()
///                  does not use it; for a scaled width of zero or less see
///                  the note. Note that height comes before width.
/// \param overwrite For an image this matters only when the list is full:
///                  a non-overwriting add to an existing row then needs a
///                  free row, so it purges or fails. addImageEntry itself
///                  always writes the cell in place and never shifts rows.
///                  Zero Hour's declaration defaults it to true.
/// \param color     The colour the image is drawn with (Zero Hour's default
///                  is opaque white).
/// \return The row written, or -1 when the list is full and does not
///         auto-purge or the position is invalid; 0 for a null or destroyed
///         window.
///
/// \note Zero Hour: the drawing code treats a width or height of zero or
/// less as the column width or the row height. BFME 2's drawing of image
/// cells is not established here.
/// \par Remastering
/// Image cell sizes are resolution-scaled: callers give sizes for a 1024 by
/// 768 screen and BFME 2 scales them. Integer division rounds the scaled
/// size toward zero.
/// \see GadgetListBoxAddEntryImage(GameWindow *, const Image *, Int, Int, Bool, Int),
///      GadgetListBoxAddEntryText(), computeTotalHeight()


/// \fn Int GadgetListBoxAddEntryImage(GameWindow *listbox, const Image *image, Int row, Int column, Bool overwrite, Int color)
/// \brief Puts an image into a list box cell without giving an image size
///        (height and width -1).
///
/// Forwards to the eight-argument overload with a height and width of -1.
/// BFME 2's resolution scaling leaves both stored values at zero or below,
/// so in computeTotalHeight() the image cell counts as one line of the list
/// box font, and the row takes the height of its tallest cell (a wrapped
/// text cell in another column can be taller). Zero Hour's drawing also
/// uses the column width or row height for such cells; BFME 2's drawing of
/// image cells is not established here.
///
/// \param listbox   The list box (see the eight-argument overload).
/// \param image     The image to show.
/// \param row       The row to write, or -1 to append.
/// \param column    The column to write, or -1 for column 0.
/// \param overwrite As for the eight-argument overload: for an image it
///                  matters only when the list is full; the cell itself is
///                  always replaced.
/// \param color     The colour the image is drawn with.
/// \return As for the eight-argument overload.
/// \see GadgetListBoxAddEntryImage(GameWindow *, const Image *, Int, Int, Int, Int, Bool, Int)


/// \fn Int GadgetSliderGetEnabledSelectedThumbBorderColor(GameWindow *slider)
/// \brief Returns a colour of a slider's thumb for the enabled pressed
///        look, or the undefined colour when the slider has no thumb.
///
/// The thumb is the slider's child window. GadgetListBoxSetColors() calls
/// it to give a list box's scroll buttons the value as their enabled
/// selected colour, so the buttons match the thumb. Zero Hour defines the function inline in GadgetSlider.h; BFME 2
/// has an out-of-line copy.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The value read is the colour field of the thumb's second enabled
/// draw-data entry, not its border colour. That reading follows the
/// draw-data layout GadgetListBoxSetColors() establishes and agrees with
/// how it uses the result. The disabled and highlighted siblings,
/// GadgetSliderGetDisabledSelectedThumbBorderColor() and
/// GadgetSliderGetHiliteSelectedThumbBorderColor(), read their states'
/// colour fields in the same way.
///
/// \param slider The slider; must not be null.
/// \return The thumb's colour, or the undefined colour when the slider has
///         no child: the value Zero Hour calls GAME_COLOR_UNDEFINED, white
///         with zero alpha.
///
/// \warning The name may not fit the body. Zero Hour's function of this
/// name returns the border colour; the field this body reads is the one
/// Zero Hour's GadgetSliderGetEnabledSelectedThumbColor returns. The name
/// is carried from the BFME 1 reconstruction.
/// \see GadgetListBoxSetColors()


/// \fn void GadgetComboBoxGetSelectedPos(GameWindow *comboBox, Int *selectedIndex)
/// \brief Reads which row of a combo box's list is selected.
///
/// Screens call this to read a choice, usually followed by
/// GadgetComboBoxGetItemData() for the value stored with that row.
/// Callers include AptOptions::Save, MapSelectMenuSystem and the
/// multiplayer game rules screen.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Sends GCM_GET_SELECTION (Zero Hour's name) with selectedIndex as the
/// second data word. BFME 2's combo box handler passes it to
/// GadgetListBoxGetSelected() on its list box, or writes -1 when the combo
/// box has no list box.
///
/// \param comboBox      The combo box; null does nothing and leaves the
///                      output unwritten.
/// \param selectedIndex [out] The selected row, or -1 when nothing is
///                      selected.
/// \see GadgetComboBoxSetSelectedPos(), GadgetComboBoxGetItemData()


/// \fn void GadgetComboBoxSetSelectedPos(GameWindow *comboBox, Int selectedIndex, Bool dontHide)
/// \brief Selects a row of a combo box's list, copying its text into the
///        edit box and notifying the combo box's owner.
///
/// Screens use this to show a stored choice, often after searching the
/// rows' item data for a value (AptMpGameRules::UpdateRuleGadget does
/// that). Other callers include AptOptions::InitGadgets, AptOptions::Reset
/// and AptMpGameSetup::PopulatePlayerCombo.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Sends GCM_SET_SELECTION (Zero Hour's name) with the index and dontHide.
/// BFME 2's combo box handler, like Zero Hour's:
/// - if dontHide is true and the drop-down list is open, remembers to keep
///   it open for this one selection;
/// - calls GadgetListBoxSetSelected() on its list box.
/// The list box, being a combo box child, reports the result back with
/// GLM_SELECTED. The combo box then closes its list (unless told to keep it
/// open). For a real row it copies the row's text and colour into the edit
/// box and sends GCM_SELECTED to its own owner; for -1 it stops there.
///
/// \param comboBox      The combo box; null does nothing.
/// \param selectedIndex The row to select, or -1 to clear the selection
///                      (the edit text is then left as it was).
/// \param dontHide      True to leave an open drop-down list open (Zero
///                      Hour's declaration defaults it to false).
///
/// \note Unlike a plain list box in BFME 2 (see
/// GadgetListBoxSetSelected(GameWindow *, Int)), a combo box selection made
/// in code does reach the combo box's owner as GCM_SELECTED, when the
/// row is valid and not empty.
/// \see GadgetComboBoxGetSelectedPos(), GadgetComboBoxHideList()


/// \fn void GadgetComboBoxHideList(GameWindow *comboBox)
/// \brief Closes a combo box's drop-down list.
///
/// EnableAcceptControls, the game setup helper that enables or disables a
/// slot's controls, calls it to close the colour and team combo boxes'
/// lists of an observer slot before disabling them.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Sends GGM_CLOSE with both data words zero. BFME 2's combo box handler
/// passes it to an internal helper that closes the list. Zero Hour: that
/// helper (HideListBox) hides the list box if it is shown and shrinks the
/// combo box back to the edit box's height.
///
/// \param comboBox The combo box; null does nothing.
/// \see GadgetComboBoxSetSelectedPos()


/// \fn void GadgetComboBoxSetItemData(GameWindow *comboBox, Int index, void *data)
/// \brief Attaches a caller-defined pointer-sized value to one row of a
///        combo box.
///
/// Screens store the meaning of a row here (an index, an identifier, an
/// address), so they can later read the choice back without depending on
/// row order. Callers include AptMpGameSetup::PopulatePlayerCombo,
/// AptMpGameRules::PopulateComboBoxes and AptOptions::InitGadgets.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Sends GCM_SET_ITEM_DATA (Zero Hour's name) with the index and the value.
/// BFME 2's combo box handler stores the value as the item data of column
/// 0 of that row in its list box (GadgetListBoxSetItemData), if the combo
/// box has a list box.
///
/// \param comboBox The combo box; null does nothing.
/// \param index    The row, normally the index GadgetComboBoxAddEntry()
///                 returned.
/// \param data     The value. The combo box never dereferences or frees it.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The value is stored as a pointer and screens put integers in it; a
/// 64-bit port must keep that round trip lossless.
/// \see GadgetComboBoxGetItemData(), GadgetComboBoxAddEntry()


/// \fn void *GadgetComboBoxGetItemData(GameWindow *comboBox, Int index)
/// \brief Returns the value attached to one row of a combo box, or null.
///
/// Callers include AptOptions::Save (which reads the chosen network
/// address this way), AptMpGameRules::UpdateRuleGadget and the
/// multiplayer game rules screen's value query.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Starts with a null result and, for a non-null combo box, sends
/// GCM_GET_ITEM_DATA (Zero Hour's name) with the index and the result's
/// address. BFME 2's combo box handler fills it from column 0 of that row of
/// its list box (GadgetListBoxGetItemData), if it has a list box.
///
/// \param comboBox The combo box; may be null.
/// \param index    The row.
/// \return The stored value; null for a null combo box, a combo box with no
///         list box, or a row with nothing stored.
/// \see GadgetComboBoxSetItemData(), GadgetComboBoxGetSelectedPos()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetComboBoxAddEntry.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_gui_listbox_combobox_api
/// \brief Adding a row to a combo box: GadgetComboBoxAddEntry().


/// \fn Int GadgetComboBoxAddEntry(GameWindow *comboBox, UnicodeString text, Color color)
/// \brief Appends a line of text to a combo box's list and resizes the
///        drop-down to fit.
///
/// The standard way to fill a combo box. Retail callers include
/// AptMpGameSetup::PopulatePlayerCombo, AptMpGameSetup::PopulateHeroCombo,
/// AptMpGameRules::PopulateComboBoxes, AptOptions::InitGadgets,
/// AptMpClans::PopulateMyClans and MapSelectMenuInit. Callers usually pair
/// it with GadgetComboBoxSetItemData() on the returned row.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Returns -1 for a null combo box; otherwise sends GCM_ADD_ENTRY (Zero
/// Hour's name) with the text's address and the colour and returns the
/// handler's result. BFME 2's combo box handler:
/// - increments the combo box's entry count and, when it reaches the list
///   box's list length, doubles that length, so the list grows as
///   needed;
/// - appends the text to its list box with GadgetListBoxAddEntryText()
///   (row -1, column 0, overwrite true);
/// - sizes the list box: as many rows as there are entries, up to the
///   combo box's maximum display count (GadgetComboBoxSetMaxDisplay()),
///   each row the list font's height plus 2 pixels, and 4 pixels more in
///   all; with no more entries than that it hides the scroll bar and its
///   buttons and makes the list 16 pixels wider than the combo box,
///   otherwise it shows them and matches the combo box's width;
/// - places the list box directly below the edit box;
/// - new in BFME 2: when the list box is enabled, enables the drop-down
///   button if there is more than one entry and disables it otherwise.
///
/// \param comboBox The combo box; null returns -1.
/// \param text     The text of the new row, copied by the list box.
/// \param color    The text colour of the new row.
/// \return The row the text was added at, or -1 for a null combo box or a
///         failed add.
///
/// \warning The handler reads the list box's data before checking that the
/// list box exists, in BFME 2 as in Zero Hour, so a combo box without a
/// list box crashes here rather than returning -1.
/// \par Modding
/// The drop-down size follows the number of entries and the maximum display
/// count from the COMBOBOXDATA block of the .wnd layout; the pixel padding
/// is fixed in code. In BFME 2 each add, when the combo box's list box is
/// enabled, enables the drop-down button only if there are two or more
/// entries; with a single entry the button stays disabled.
/// \see GadgetComboBoxReset(), GadgetComboBoxSetItemData(),
///      GadgetComboBoxSetSelectedPos(), GadgetListBoxAddEntryText()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetButtonSetText.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_gui_listbox_combobox_api
/// \brief Setting a push button's label: GadgetButtonSetText().
///
/// The file also holds other code that this group does not document.


/// \fn void GadgetButtonSetText(GameWindow *g, UnicodeString text)
/// \brief Sets the label text of a push button.
///
/// Retail callers include ControlBar::setControlCommand (command button
/// labels), InGameUI::hideIdleWorkerLayout (which blanks a button) and
/// setWindowText, the window layout loader's helper that applies a
/// window's text label.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Sends GGM_SET_LABEL with the text's address and zero. BFME 2's push
/// button handler calls the base GameWindow::winSetText directly, not
/// through the virtual table. Zero Hour's makes a virtual winSetText call,
/// which Zero Hour's W3DGameWindow overrides to rebuild its text renderer
/// as well. Other gadget types answer GGM_SET_LABEL in their own way;
/// BFME 2's combo box handler, for example, writes the text straight into
/// the window's instance data instead of calling GameWindow::winSetText.
///
/// \param g    The button; null does nothing.
/// \param text The new label, copied.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The label is whatever the caller passes. A push button's text from a
/// .wnd layout arrives through setWindowText, which in BFME 2 as in Zero
/// Hour first looks the label up in the string table (TheGameText), so
/// the wording of such buttons lives in the string table. In BFME 2,
/// ControlBar::setControlCommand blanks the label of a command button that
/// is drawn with an image.
/// \see GadgetPushButtonSystem
